I've found relevant information about the XIAO ESP32S3 Sense onboard microphone configuration. Here are the key resources and code examples:

## Basic Microphone Example

For the XIAO ESP32S3 Sense microphone, you can use the I2S library with `I2S.setAllPins(-1, 42, 41, -1, -1)` to initialize I2S at 16 kHz with 16-bits per sample in PDM_MONO_MODE. Here's the complete setup:

```cpp
#include <I2S.h>

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(115200);
    while (!Serial) { }

    // setup 42 PDM clock and 41 PDM data pins
    I2S.setAllPins(-1, 42, 41, -1, -1);

    if (!I2S.begin(PDM_MONO_MODE, 16000, 16)) {
        Serial.println("Failed to initialize I2S!");
        while (1);
    }
}

void loop() {
    // read a sample
    int sample = I2S.read();
    if (sample && sample != -1 && sample != 1) {
        Serial.println(sample);
    }
}
```

## Alternative Method Using setPinsPdmRx

A newer approach uses `I2S.setPinsPdmRx(42, 41)` with `I2S.begin(I2S_MODE_PDM_RX, 16000, I2S_DATA_BIT_WIDTH_16BIT, I2S_SLOT_MODE_MONO)`.

## Pin Configuration

The `setAllPins()` function takes five integer parameters representing GPIO pins for the I2S interface: bit clock, word select, data input, data output, and channel select lines, respectively. For the XIAO ESP32S3 Sense, GPIO 42 is the PDM clock and GPIO 41 is the PDM data line.

Both methods work with the built-in microphone for reading PDM audio samples via I2S.


